.TH GXWI-SECURITY-POLICY 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-security-policy 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-security-policy \- see and change what this machine trusts on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-security-policy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Security Policy shows what this machine trusts: the certificate authorities every program accepts, what this machine has added to them and what it refuses, and the keys the kernel verifies code signatures with.
It is started from the launcher, and takes no arguments.
.PP
Its sections are down the side.
Trusted lists every certificate authority in force, shipped with Peios or added here; opening one shows who issued it, when it is valid, its fingerprint and what it is trusted for, and offers to export it as a PEM file, distrust it, or remove one this machine added.
Add Certificate opens a PEM or DER file and shows what is in it before anything is trusted: one that is not a CA, has expired, or is already trusted or distrusted is refused with the reason.
Distrusted lists what is refused, to trust again, and refuses a certificate by its fingerprint.
Settings shows how trustd is faring, with Reload, turns the certificate files under
.I /etc/ssl
on and off, and opens the permissions editor on who can change trust and who can control trustd.
Signing Keys lists the kernel's code-signing keys, which are compiled into the kernel: nothing here changes them.
.PP
It reads from trustd, and changes the registry keys trustd reads under
.BR Machine\eSystem\eTrust ,
as the person, taking the same steps as
.BR trust (1).
As shipped, only Administrators can change what this machine trusts; anyone else can look, and the window says once why they cannot change it.
Reload is offered to those trustd's own permissions let reload it.
Anything that widens or narrows what every program accepts asks first.
.PP
The window follows trustd, so a change made with
.BR trust (1)
or
.BR reg (1)
appears within a few seconds.
It needs a desktop: run with no GXWI_DISPLAY, it says so and ends.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Machine trust configuration is stored under Machine\eSystem\eTrust and consumed by trustd.
Its registry manual owns the configuration schema.
.SH SECURITY
Trust changes require write access to the corresponding registry settings.
Reload uses trustd's separate control right.
The kernel signing-key list is read-only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-security-policy.toml
Its application declaration, which lists it in the launcher.
.TP
.I /run/trustd/trust.sock
trustd's socket, where the certificates in force and its status are read.
.TP
.I /usr/share/ca-certificates/mozilla.crt
The certificates Peios ships, read to name a distrusted one.
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/security/kacs/signing_keys
The kernel's code-signing keys.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trust (1),
.BR trustd (8),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1),
.BR gxwi-file-dialog (1).
.PP
Adding and distrusting certificates, and Keys and image build, at learn.peios.org.
